<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rastislav of Moravia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–<a href="/years/870">870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saint Rastislav</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rastislav was ruler of Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> from 846 to 870. Installed by the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, he soon asserted his independence, building strong fortresses and resisting Frankish attacks. He asked the Byzantine emperor Michael III for missionaries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> arrived in 863 to teach in the Slavic language. Betrayed by his nephew Svatopluk, he was handed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and blinded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rastislav-of-moravia.jpg" alt="Rastislav of Moravia" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Rastislav.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, Rastislav was a nephew of Mojmir I, the first known ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. His career before 846 is unknown, though he may have served as a hostage for his uncle at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>. Louis invaded Moravia in 846, removed Mojmir I and installed Rastislav as duke. He was probably already Christian, but was baptised by 846 at the latest, as a condition of the king's support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For his first eight years Rastislav seems to have stayed loyal to Louis, while acquiring territory in the east and setting a border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>. By 855 he felt strong enough to challenge Frankish overlordship. In 855 Louis invaded with a large army but failed before one of Rastislav's strongholds, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulcice">Mikulčice</a>, and withdrew, while Moravian forces pillaged Frankish estates on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Louis's son Carloman led a failed expedition in 858 and later allied with Rastislav against his father. In August 864 Louis besieged the fortress of Dowina, perhaps Devín Castle, took Rastislav by surprise and forced him to give hostages and swear a new oath, though this did not subdue him.</p><p>Around 862 Rastislav, hoping to limit the Frankish missionaries in his realm, turned first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for teachers able to work in the Slavic language. The Byzantine emperor Michael III sent the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, who arrived in 863, taught and said mass in Slavonic, and Cyril created a script for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. Frankish clergy saw a threat in their work, but they enjoyed Rastislav's protection. In late 866 they left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Rome, where Pope Hadrian II approved their Slavic translations of the Scriptures. In 868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> burned Rastislav's fortifications.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rastislav's nephew Svatopluk negotiated with Carloman and accepted his lordship without Rastislav's knowledge. Rastislav is said to have been enraged and to have plotted to have him strangled at a banquet, but Svatopluk was warned, captured his uncle and sent him in bonds to Carloman. At Regensburg in 870 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> condemned Rastislav to death for treason, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> commuted the sentence to blinding and imprisonment. He died in prison. The Czech and Slovak Orthodox Church canonised him in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rastislav of Moravia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Great Moravia. Died in Regensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=870" aria-label="Regensburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Rastislav of Moravia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 5872h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 5872h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=870">What was here in 870 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
